# Line ending policy.
#
# Git can be told to rewrite the line endings of text files when they are
# checked out, so that they match whatever the local operating system prefers.
# On Windows that means turning every "\n" into "\r\n", and the Git for Windows
# installer turns this on by default (it sets core.autocrlf=true). Without the
# rules in this file, whether a given file has carriage returns in it depends
# on which machine last checked it out.
#
# For most of this repository that would be harmless. For the lib directory it
# is not, for two reasons:
#
#   - Those files are packed verbatim into an archive that is embedded inside
#     the ego executable (see internal/cli/app/library.go), and unpacked
#     verbatim onto the user's disk. Carriage returns introduced at checkout
#     time on a build machine are therefore shipped to every user of that
#     build. The help text is matched line by line against exact strings, so
#     the result was that the "help" command in the interactive console could
#     not find any topic at all.
#
#   - The archive is meant to be reproducible: building it twice from the same
#     source must produce identical bytes. If the working copy differs between
#     a Windows checkout and a Unix one, so does the archive.
#
# "text eol=lf" tells Git to store these files with line feeds, as it always
# has, and to also write line feeds into the working tree on every platform.

lib/**  text eol=lf

# The dashboard's images are not text, and must never have anything rewritten
# inside them. This rule comes after the one above because when two patterns
# match the same file, the last one wins.

lib/**/*.png  binary

# The message catalogs are read by the tools/lang generator and compiled into
# the executable, so they get the same treatment for the same reasons.

internal/i18n/languages/*.txt  text eol=lf

# Go source is not affected by any of this -- the compiler accepts either
# convention -- but keeping it consistent means "git diff" never reports that
# every line of a file changed just because it was edited on another platform.

*.go    text eol=lf
